;; Docs for text/bjodat-core.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "bjodat-core.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "The bjodat value, its reader and its writer: Bjolang's shapes read as data.")
  (see Bjodat parse-bjodat bjodat->string bjodat-ref)
  (example """
    (import (text bjodat-core))

    (parse-bjodat "{:a 1 :b [2 3]}")     ; (Ok (BjoMap ...))
    (parse-bjodat-all "1 2 3")           ; (Ok (list (BjoInt 1) ...)), a file is a stream
    (bjodat->string v)                   ; the canonical spelling, maps flat
    """)
  (reference """"
    bjodat is Bjolang's shapes read as @em{data}: no evaluation, no macro
    expansion, no environment. A file is what it says it is. It is what a
    package manifest, a lockfile or a config file is written in.

    This module is the machinery, not the interface. A program that declares
    records imports @code{(text bjodat)}: its @code{def/bjodat-type} reads the
    text straight into a record, with no @code{Bjodat} value in between, and
    it re-exports @code{Bjodat}, its cases, @code{Reader}, the one-pass
    driver and @code{bjodat->string}, so that one import covers the
    generator, the traits and the writer. Import this module by name when you
    want the @em{tree}: @code{parse-bjodat}, @code{read-bjodat},
    @code{bjodat-ref} and the entry accessors, for data whose shape is not
    known until run time.

    @strong{The format.} Two differences from source, and they are the whole
    of it:

    @itemlist{
      @item{A bare identifier is a @code{Symbol}. It is already quoted,
      because there is nothing here to evaluate and so nothing for a quote to
      protect it from. @code{'foo} is @em{refused}: the mark would be a second
      spelling of one value.}
      @item{@code{{...}} is a map. In source a brace opens a comprehension,
      and a comprehension is a loop, and a loop is not data.}
    }

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    42  -100  3.14159  1e10  0x2A  0b101010   ;; integer or double, and nothing else
    #t  #f                                    ;; the booleans; `true` is a symbol
    #\a  #\newline  #\space                   ;; characters, as in source
    "a \"quoted\" word\n"                     ;; strings, escaped as source escapes
    """a "raw" word"""                        ;; raw strings, as source writes them
    :key   #:key                              ;; a keyword, either spelling
    a-symbol   bjolang/http   net8.0          ;; symbols; `.` and `/` are in a name
    (a list "of" :anything)                   ;; a list
    ["a" "vec" "of" "strings"]                ;; a vec, the one sequence type
    {:name "http" :version "1.2"}             ;; a map, flat
    {(:name "http") (:version "1.2")}         ;; a map, parenthesised
    ;; a comment, to the end of the line
    }|

    The escapes are those source has: @code{\n}, @code{\t}, @code{\r},
    @code{\\}, @code{\"}, @code{\0} and @code{\uXXXX}. The named characters
    are @code{#\newline}, @code{#\space}, @code{#\tab}, @code{#\return} and
    @code{#\nul}. A byte order mark at the start of the text is skipped.

    A raw string follows the rules in @code{Syntax.org}, "Raw strings",
    margin and all. The writer still writes every string escaped and on one
    line: a raw string written back out would need a margin to fit into the
    text around it, and @code{bjodat->string} writes a value on its own.

    A number has no width suffix. @code{21uy} and @code{21L} name a .NET
    type, and which type a field holds is the record's to say, not the file's.
    A number is a @code{long} unless it has a fraction or an exponent, or is
    too wide for a @code{long}, and then it is a @code{double}. @code{-} is a
    symbol of its own, and the start of a number when a digit follows it.

    There is no @code{#[...]}. Source splits vec from array because one is
    immutable and the other is a .NET array; data is neither, so a second
    spelling would read the same and mean nothing.

    A comma is not whitespace. Source lexes @code{,} as unquote, so a stray
    one is a token rather than a decoration, and it is refused. Separate with
    spaces.

    @strong{Maps.} Two spellings, and no third. Which one a map is, is
    decided by the character after the brace: a @code{(} means the
    parenthesised form, anything else means the flat one. They do not mix,
    and a map that starts one way and continues the other is refused.

    That rule is why @em{a list may not be a key}. If it could,
    @code{{(a b) 1}} would be both a flat map keyed by the list
    @code{(a b)} and a parenthesised map whose first entry is @code{a -> b}.
    A key is a name, and a name is a keyword, a symbol, a string, a number
    or a char, never a list. A flat map with an odd number of forms is the
    other refusal.

    Entries keep the order they were written in, and writing one back out
    writes them in that order. A manifest that round-trips through a record
    comes back looking like what a person wrote, not sorted by hash.

    @strong{A file is a stream.} Nothing requires a file to hold exactly one
    value, which is what makes a log or a lockfile writable by appending.
    @code{parse-bjodat} asks for exactly one and refuses trailing text;
    @code{parse-bjodat-all} takes them all, and an empty file is an empty
    list rather than an error.

    @strong{The tree.} A @code{BjoMap} holds a flat @code{(Vec Bjodat)} of
    alternating key and value, in written order, not a trie. Building a trie
    was about seventy percent of what reading a keyed document cost, and a
    record decoded from one reads every field once and throws it away. Reach
    into it with @code{bjodat-entry-count}, @code{bjodat-entry-key},
    @code{bjodat-entry-value} and @code{bjodat-ref}. If you want a
    dictionary, build one from the entries.

    @strong{Refusals.} Every reader answers @code{(Result string _)}. A
    refusal while reading carries the line and column where it was found:

    @codeblock[#:lang "text"]|{
    line 1, column 12: Package: this map is written flat, so a '(' here is an entry of the other form
    }|

    @strong{The one-pass machinery.} @code{Reader} and the functions from
    @code{open-map!} to @code{parse-one-pass-all} are what a decoder
    generated by @code{def/bjodat-type} drives to read a record without a
    @code{Bjodat} tree standing between the text and the fields. They are
    small and awkward on purpose: the caller is generated code that already
    knows its field names, and drives the loop itself rather than handing in
    a table, because a table would be the thing this is trying not to build.
    They are not an API for people, but the entry points
    (@code{parse-one-pass} and the rest) are how a generated
    @code{bjodat-read-<Name>} is run.
    """"))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The value
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Bjodat
  (summary "A bjodat value: what a document reads as, as a tree.")
  (case BjoBool "@code{#t} or @code{#f}.")
  (case BjoInt "An integer, always a @code{long}.")
  (case BjoFloat "A number with a fraction or an exponent, or one too wide for a @code{long}.")
  (case BjoStr "A string, raw or escaped.")
  (case BjoChar "A character, @code{#\\a} or @code{#\\newline}.")
  (case BjoSym "A bare identifier. It is data, so it needs no quote.")
  (case BjoKey "A keyword, written @code{:key} or @code{#:key}.")
  (case BjoList "A @code{(...)}.")
  (case BjoVec "A @code{[...]}.")
  (case BjoMap "A @code{{...}}: key, value, key, value, an even length, in written order, whichever spelling it was written in.")
  (see parse-bjodat bjodat->string bjodat-ref)
  (example """
    (match (parse-bjodat "{:name \"http\"}")
      ((Ok (BjoMap entries)) (bjodat-ref entries :name))   ; (Some (BjoStr "http"))
      (_ None))
    """)
  (reference """
    A @code{BjoMap} is not a trie or a dictionary but a flat vec of
    alternating key and value. Read it with @code{bjodat-entry-count},
    @code{bjodat-entry-key}, @code{bjodat-entry-value} and
    @code{bjodat-ref}.
    """))

(:doc bjodat-max-depth
  (summary "How deep a value may nest before the reader refuses it: 200.")
  (see read-bjodat)
  (reference """
    It applies to nesting in the reader, so a hostile document nested a
    thousand deep is refused with a message rather than by the stack. Each
    record a generated one-pass decoder reads starts a fresh budget, so a
    chain of nested @em{record types} is bounded by the types rather than by
    this; the values inside a field are bounded normally.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Reading and writing
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc read-bjodat
  (summary "Reads exactly one value from a port, and then the end.")
  (arg port "The port to read from, to its end.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok value)}, or @code{(Err message)} naming the line and column. Text after the value is refused.")
  (see parse-bjodat read-bjodat-all))

(:doc parse-bjodat
  (summary "Reads exactly one value from a string.")
  (arg text "The document.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok value)}, or @code{(Err message)} naming the line and column. Text after the value is refused.")
  (see read-bjodat parse-bjodat-all bjodat->string)
  (example """
    (parse-bjodat "{:a 1 :b [2 3]}")   ; (Ok (BjoMap ...))
    (parse-bjodat "1 2")               ; (Err "line 1, column 3: the value ended here, ...")
    """))

(:doc read-bjodat-all
  (summary "Reads every value from a port, in order.")
  (arg port "The port to read from, to its end.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok values)}, or the first refusal. An empty document is the empty list.")
  (see parse-bjodat-all read-bjodat)
  (reference """
    A file is a stream of values, which is what makes a log or a lockfile
    writable by appending.
    """))

(:doc parse-bjodat-all
  (summary "Reads every value in a string, in order.")
  (arg text "The document.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok values)}, or the first refusal. An empty document is the empty list.")
  (see read-bjodat-all parse-bjodat))

(:doc bjodat->string
  (summary "Writes a value in the canonical spelling.")
  (arg v "The value.")
  (returns "Its text: maps flat, entries in the order they are held, strings escaped and on one line.")
  (see parse-bjodat)
  (example """
    (bjodat->string (BjoVec [(BjoInt 1) (BjoSym 'a)]))   ; "[1 a]"
    """)
  (reference """
    Strings are always written escaped, never raw: a raw string written back
    out would need a margin to fit into the text around it. A double is
    written as .NET writes it, so one with no fraction, such as @code{2.0},
    is written @code{2} and reads back as a @code{BjoInt}; the
    @code{double} instance of @code{bjodat->} in @code{(text bjodat)}
    takes an integer for that reason. Symbols and keywords are written as
    their names, unchecked.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; What a map is, for anyone holding one
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc bjodat-entry-count
  (summary "How many entries a map's vec holds.")
  (arg entries "What a @code{BjoMap} holds.")
  (returns "Half its length.")
  (see bjodat-entry-key bjodat-entry-value))

(:doc bjodat-entry-key
  (summary "The key of a map's i-th entry.")
  (arg entries "What a @code{BjoMap} holds.")
  (arg i "The entry, from 0.")
  (returns "Its key, which may be any value but a list.")
  (see bjodat-entry-value bjodat-entry-count))

(:doc bjodat-entry-value
  (summary "The value of a map's i-th entry.")
  (arg entries "What a @code{BjoMap} holds.")
  (arg i "The entry, from 0.")
  (returns "Its value.")
  (see bjodat-entry-key bjodat-entry-count))

(:doc bjodat-ref
  (summary "The first value under a keyword in a map.")
  (arg entries "What a @code{BjoMap} holds.")
  (arg k "The keyword.")
  (returns "@code{(Some value)} for the first entry under @code{k}, in written order, or @code{None}.")
  (see bjodat-entry-count)
  (example """
    (bjodat-ref entries :version)
    """)
  (reference """
    A linear scan comparing interned keywords by reference. That is the
    right shape for a record's handful of fields and the wrong one for a map
    with thousands of keys used as a lookup table: if you want a dictionary,
    build one from the entries. Only a @code{BjoKey} key can match, so an
    entry under a symbol or a string is never found. A duplicate key: the
    first wins.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; What a generated one-pass decoder drives. Not an API for people.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Reader
  (summary "A reader over a port, as a generated one-pass decoder drives it. Not an API for people.")
  (field port "What it reads from.")
  (field held "The one character of lookahead, as a code; -1 at the end of the port.")
  (field row "The line the held character is on, from 1.")
  (field col "Its column, from 1.")
  (field scratch "One buffer for every string, symbol, number and key name in the document.")
  (field stack "One array for every list, vec and map in the document.")
  (field top "How much of the stack is in use.")
  (field names "A small cache of the names read, so that a repeated key does not build a new string.")
  (see read-one-pass parse-one-pass)
  (reference """
    Made by @code{read-one-pass} and its neighbours and handed to the
    decoder they are given. A program has no reason to touch its fields.
    """))

(:doc open-map!
  (summary "Steps over the brace that opens a map, and says which spelling it is in. Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok #t)} when the entries are parenthesised, @code{(Ok #f)} when flat; a refusal when there is no map here.")
  (see next-key! end-entry!)
  (reference """
    Decided by the first thing inside the brace, as the tree reader decides
    it; every entry after has to agree.
    """))

(:doc next-key!
  (summary "Takes the next key's name into the buffer, without interning it. Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg paired "What @code{open-map!} answered.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok #t)} when a key is in the buffer and the reader stands at its value, @code{(Ok #f)} when the map closed.")
  (see key=? end-entry! skip-value!)
  (reference """
    A key that is not a keyword is read and dropped, and leaves the buffer
    empty, which no field name can equal: it takes the path an unknown key
    takes, as in the tree decoder, where @code{bjodat-ref} only matches a
    @code{BjoKey}.
    """))

(:doc end-entry!
  (summary "Steps over the ) of a parenthesised entry; nothing in the flat form. Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg paired "What @code{open-map!} answered.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok #t)}, or a refusal when the entry holds more than a key and a value.")
  (see next-key!))

(:doc key=?
  (summary "Whether the name in the reader's buffer is this one. Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader.")
  (arg name "A field name or tag, without its colon.")
  (returns "#t when they are equal.")
  (see next-key! next-field!)
  (reference """
    The comparison the one-pass decoder exists for: no string is built and
    nothing is interned, for a key, a clause name or a tag.
    """))

(:doc field-value
  (summary "Reads a field's value, as a Bjodat for its type's instance to convert. Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader, standing at the value.")
  (returns "The value, or a refusal.")
  (see skip-value!)
  (reference """
    The value is still read as a @code{Bjodat} and converted by the field
    type's own @code{bjodat->} instance. That keeps every instance, including
    one a user wrote, working, and every message identical to the tree
    decoder's. The entry is what the one-pass decoder saves, not the value.
    """))

(:doc skip-value!
  (summary "Reads an unknown key's value and drops it. Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader, standing at the value.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok #t)}, or a refusal.")
  (see field-value skip-clause!)
  (reference """
    It has to be @em{read}: a value is not a token, and finding where it ends
    means parsing it.
    """))

;; The same, for a record written as a tagged form.

(:doc open-form!
  (summary "Steps over ( and a tagged form's head, refusing any head but the tag. Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg tag "The head the record is written under.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok #t)}, or a refusal such as @code{expected (package ...), and this is (banana ...)}.")
  (see next-field! skip-clause!)
  (reference """
    The tag is matched against the buffer, so a form costs no string for its
    own name; one is built only on the way to refusing.
    """))

(:doc next-field!
  (summary "Takes the next clause's field name into the buffer. Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok #t)} when a name is in the buffer and the reader stands at the clause's first value, @code{(Ok #f)} when the form closed.")
  (see key=? end-clause! skip-clause! clause-values!))

(:doc end-clause!
  (summary "Steps over the ) of a clause holding one value. Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader.")
  (arg owner "The record's name, for messages.")
  (arg name "The field's name, for messages.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok #t)}, or a refusal when the clause holds more than one value.")
  (see next-field!)
  (reference """
    The owner and the field name arrive apart and are joined only on the way
    to refusing: joined by the caller they would be a string built per field
    per record.
    """))

(:doc skip-clause!
  (summary "Reads an unknown clause to its ), whatever it holds, and drops it. Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader, after the clause's name.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok #t)}, or a refusal.")
  (see skip-value!)
  (reference """
    Any arity, and that is the point of it. A map's unknown key is one value
    and done, but a clause may hold none, one or five, and a reader that
    insisted on one would make every new clause a breaking change for every
    older tool. This is what keeps a manifest forward-compatible.
    """))

(:doc clause-values!
  (summary "Reads every value of a clause, for a #:rest field, and its ). Not an API for people.")
  (arg rd "The reader, after the clause's name.")
  (returns "The values in written order, or a refusal.")
  (see next-field! field-value)
  (reference """
    The values are read as @code{Bjodat} and converted by the element type's
    own instance. @code{read-value} carries the depth budget, so a manifest
    nesting @code{(package (depends (package (depends ...))))} five hundred
    deep is refused by @code{bjodat-max-depth} rather than by the stack. A
    @code{#:rest} that recursed straight back into a generated decoder would
    have no budget at all.
    """))

;; The entry points.

(:doc read-one-pass
  (summary "Runs a one-pass decoder over a port: one record, and then the end.")
  (arg port "The port to read from, to its end.")
  (arg decode "A generated @code{bjodat-read-<Name>}.")
  (returns "What the decoder answered; text after the record is refused.")
  (see parse-one-pass read-one-pass-all)
  (reference """
    The decoder is typed @code{-?->}, so that a bjoroutine calling this gets
    the decoder's suspending copy rather than the one that parks the thread.
    Pass it by name, not wrapped in a @code{(fun (rd) ...)}: a lambda body is
    sealed to the sync copy of what it calls.
    """))

(:doc parse-one-pass
  (summary "Runs a one-pass decoder over a string: one record, and then the end.")
  (arg text "The document.")
  (arg decode "A generated @code{bjodat-read-<Name>}, passed by name.")
  (returns "What the decoder answered; text after the record is refused.")
  (see read-one-pass parse-one-pass-all)
  (reference """
    A generated @code{bjodat-parse-<Name>} is exactly this with its own
    decoder.
    """))

(:doc read-one-pass-all
  (summary "Runs a one-pass decoder over each record of a [...] read from a port.")
  (arg port "The port to read from.")
  (arg decode "A generated @code{bjodat-read-<Name>}, passed by name.")
  (returns "The records in order, or the first refusal. The document has to be a vec.")
  (see parse-one-pass-all read-one-pass)
  (reference """
    A @code{List} rather than a @code{Vec}: it is built by consing and
    turned round once, and a caller that folds over it once, which is most
    of them, should not pay for a tree it does not index into.
    """))

(:doc parse-one-pass-all
  (summary "Runs a one-pass decoder over each record of a [...] in a string.")
  (arg text "The document, a vec of records.")
  (arg decode "A generated @code{bjodat-read-<Name>}, passed by name.")
  (returns "The records in order, or the first refusal.")
  (see read-one-pass-all parse-one-pass)
  (example """
    (parse-one-pass-all text bjodat-read-Dep)   ; (Result string (List Dep))
    """))
